  "id": "Compound::DB00816 - Gene::154",
  "verbalization": "Orciprenaline, a member of the adrenergic beta2-agonists class, is directly linked to the receptor Adrb2 through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Orciprenaline, as part of the broader adrenergic beta2-agonists family, binds to Adrb2, with specific compounds like Pirbuterol and Formoterol also exhibiting this binding property. This indicates a common mechanism of action involving the activation of Adrb2 through agonist binding, which is a well-established pathway in the context of beta-adrenergic receptor signaling. The presence of multiple compounds within the same class further supports the notion that Orciprenaline shares a structural or functional relationship with Adrb2, likely through a common mechanism of receptor activation. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of adrenergic receptors in physiological responses, with Orciprenaline serving as a representative member of the class that interacts with Adrb2.",
